<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tripartite Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Berlin Pact · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tripartite Pact was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 27 September 1940 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, who pledged to assist one another if attacked by a power not yet in the war, chiefly aimed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> joined, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> signed on 25 March 1941. Two days later a military coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> overthrew the Yugoslav government, and Germany invaded the country in April.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tripartite-pact.jpg" alt="Tripartite Pact" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Hoffmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signing_ceremony_for_the_Axis_Powers_Tripartite_Pact.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had technically been allies since the Anti-Comintern Pact of 1936, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> of 1939 between Germany and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> came as a surprise to Japan. In November 1939 the two signed an Agreement for Cultural Cooperation, which restored what has been called their reluctant alliance. The Tripartite Pact, also known as the Berlin Pact, was signed in Berlin by Joachim von Ribbentrop for Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ciano">Galeazzo Ciano</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Saburō Kurusu for Japan, in the presence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Under Article 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> recognised the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in establishing a new order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and under Article 2 they recognised Japan's leadership in a new order in Greater East Asia. Article 3 bound the three to assist one another with all political, economic and military means if one was attacked by a power not at present involved in the European War or the Japanese-Chinese conflict. Article 5 left unchanged each signatory's existing relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. The pact was to last ten years.</p><p>It was directed chiefly at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, but its practical effects were limited, since the Italo-German and Japanese theatres lay on opposite sides of the world and the partners' strategic interests differed. Its defensive clauses were never invoked. Joint technical commissions were agreed on 20 December 1940, yet the military commission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> met only two or three times by 1943, and there were no trilateral naval talks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> joined on 20 November 1940, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on 23 November, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> on 24 November and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> on 1 March 1941. Hungary had first sent notice of spiritual adherence, after Ribbentrop's speech seemed to invite new members, though Ciano opposed adding smaller states. Bulgaria signed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> needed to move troops through it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> signed on 25 March 1941, and the military coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> two days later was followed by invasion by Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Hungary. The Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, created from conquered Yugoslav territory, signed on 15 June 1941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> declined to join, and German talks with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> about joining went nowhere.</p><p>On 11 December 1941, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the three powers agreed not to make a separate peace with the United States or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The historian Paul W. Schroeder describes the pact as virtually inoperative by December 1941. Its symbolic effect was larger: it persuaded the American public that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was acting in concert with Germany, and the charge that it coordinated aggression was part of the case at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and at the Tokyo trials.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tripartite Pact” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under heavy German pressure, the government of Dragiša Cvetković signed the <b>Tripartite Pact</b> in Vienna on 25 March 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A German song set to the tune replaced &quot;Giovinezza&quot; with &quot;Hitlerleute&quot;, and Japanese translations were made to mark the <b>Tripartite Pact</b> and used in Japan's overseas broadcasting.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li><li><blockquote>Romania signed the <b>Tripartite Pact</b>, the alliance concluded by Germany, Italy and Japan in Berlin on 27 September, and so formally joined the Axis powers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
